memcheck lets an undefined operand flow into a division's quotient
without a word: it reports an undefined value only where it decides a
branch or forms an address. An integer division takes a time that
depends on its operands, so a division of or by a secret leaks it,
and a taint trace should say so.

This adds an error kind, UninitOperand ("Integer division depends on
uninitialised value(s)", suppressed as "Operand"), with its origin
tracked as a condition's is, and reports it for each undefined
operand of every integer division the guest's code is lifted to:
DivModU/S128to64, 64to32, 64to64, and 32to32 (x86_64's DIV and IDIV
among them), DivU/S32 and 64 and their E forms (aarch64's UDIV and
SDIV), and the 128-bit divisions and modulos. Unlike the checks
before an address or a branch, it leaves the operands undefined
after reporting them, so the quotient's undefinedness, and what is
reported downstream of it, is as it would have been.

